.TH pass-env 1 "2026 June 30" "Version 0.2.5" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
pass-env \- a password-store extension to manage environment variables from encrypted .env entries

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pass env
.I SUBCOMMAND
[
.I ENTRY ...
] [
.B \-\-
.I COMMAND ...
]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pass-env
is an extension for
.BR pass (1)
that lets you store
.I KEY=VALUE
environment variable files as encrypted entries in the password store and
load, unload, or run commands with those variables in a single step.

Entries must end in
.B .env
(e.g.\&
.IR os/prod.env ,
.IR api/openai.env ).
Each entry contains one
.I KEY=VALUE
pair per line; blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored.

A companion shell function,
.BR passenv ,
is provided in
.I contrib/pass-env-init.sh
for sourcing into
.BR bash (1)
or
.BR zsh (1)
sessions. It wraps the pass extension and maintains a session tracker so that
loaded entries can be unloaded cleanly.

.SH PASS EXTENSION SUBCOMMANDS

.TP
.B pass env list
Print all
.I .env
entries available in the password store, one per line (no
.I .gpg
suffix), sorted alphabetically.

.TP
.BI "pass env run " "ENTRY [ENTRY ...]" " \-\- " "COMMAND [ARGS...]"
Decrypt one or more entries and execute
.I COMMAND
with the resulting variables set in its environment.  Nothing is written to
disk and no variable leaks into the calling shell.  This mode provides
isolation and cleanup (variables are scoped to the subprocess), but does not
protect against a compromised store: a tampered entry can still set dangerous
variables like
.BR PATH " or " LD_PRELOAD " to hijack the spawned process."
.PP
.RS
.EX
pass env run os/prod.env \-\- printenv MY_VAR
pass env run api/openai.env db/prod.env \-\- myapp
.EE
.RE

.TP
.BI "pass env set " "ENTRY [ENTRY ...]"
Decrypt one or more entries and print
.B export
.I KEY=VALUE
statements to stdout.  Eval the output to load variables into the current
shell.  If
.I ENTRY
is omitted and
.BR fzf (1)
is installed, an interactive picker is launched (TAB to multi-select).
.PP
.RS
.EX
eval "$(pass env set os/prod.env)"
eval "$(pass env set os/prod.env api/openai.env)"
.EE
.RE

.TP
.BI "pass env unset " "ENTRY [ENTRY ...]"
Decrypt one or more entries and print an
.B unset
statement listing all their variable names to stdout.  Eval the output to
remove the variables from the current shell.  If
.I ENTRY
is omitted and
.BR fzf (1)
is installed, an interactive picker is launched.
.PP
.RS
.EX
eval "$(pass env unset os/prod.env)"
.EE
.RE

.TP
.BR "pass env version"
Print the installed version of pass-env.

.TP
.BR "pass env help"
Print usage information for the extension.

.SH PASSENV SHELL FUNCTION

Source
.I contrib/pass-env-init.sh
in your
.I ~/.bashrc
or
.I ~/.zshrc
to enable the
.B passenv
shell function:

.RS
.EX
source /path/to/pass-env/contrib/pass-env-init.sh
.EE
.RE

The shell function wraps the pass extension, evaluates export/unset output
directly into the calling shell, and tracks loaded entries in the session
variable
.B _PASSENV_TRACKER
so that they can be unloaded by name.

.TP
.BI "passenv set " "[--force] [ENTRY ...]"
Load one or more entries into the current shell.  If no
.I ENTRY
is given, launches an interactive
.BR fzf (1)
picker.  If an entry is already loaded, it is skipped with a message unless
.B --force
is given, which forces a reload (re-decrypting the entry).
.PP
.RS
.EX
passenv set os/prod.env
passenv set os/prod.env api/openai.env
passenv set --force os/prod.env
.EE
.RE

.TP
.BI "passenv unset " "[ENTRY ...]"
Unset the variables loaded from each named entry and remove it from the
tracker.  If no
.I ENTRY
is given, launches an interactive
.BR fzf (1)
picker over currently loaded entries.

.TP
.BI "passenv run " "[ENTRY ...]" " \-\- " "COMMAND [ARGS...]"
Decrypt one or more entries and execute
.I COMMAND
with their variables set in its environment only.
Nothing leaks into the current shell.
This is a convenience wrapper around
.B pass env run
for users who have sourced the shell function.
It provides isolation and cleanup but does not protect against a compromised
store.
If no
.I ENTRY
is given before
.BR \-\- ,
an interactive
.BR fzf (1)
picker is launched.
.PP
.RS
.EX
passenv run os/prod.env \-\- printenv MY_VAR
passenv run api/openai.env db/prod.env \-\- myapp
.EE
.RE

.TP
.B passenv version
Print the installed version of pass-env.

.TP
.B passenv list
List all
.I .env
entries available in the password store (delegates to
.BR "pass env list" ).

.TP
.B passenv loaded
Print a table of all entries currently loaded in this shell session and
their associated variable names.

.TP
.B passenv help
Print usage information for the shell function.

.SH ENTRY FORMAT

Each entry is a plain-text file (encrypted with GPG by pass) containing one
.I KEY=VALUE
pair per line.
.PP
.EX
# Example entry: api/openai.env
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-abc123
OPENAI_ORG=org-xyz
.EE
.PP
Rules:
.IP \(bu 4
Blank lines are ignored.
.IP \(bu 4
Lines beginning with
.B #
are treated as comments and ignored. Only lines with
.B #
in column 0 are recognized as comments; leading whitespace before
.B #
will cause a parse error.
.IP \(bu 4
Key names must match
.B ^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$
.IP \(bu 4
Well-known dangerous environment variables (e.g.,
.BR PATH ,
.BR LD_PRELOAD ,
.BR PROMPT_COMMAND ,
.BR BASH_ENV )
are refused as defense-in-depth against a compromised store.
.IP \(bu 4
Entry filenames must end in
.B .env
(the
.I .gpg
suffix is added by pass automatically).
.IP \(bu 4
Values cannot span multiple lines.
Newlines within values are not supported; a line that does not match
.I KEY=VALUE
format triggers an error.
.IP \(bu 4
Symlinked entries are supported.  A symlink inside the store that resolves
outside the store will be rejected.

.SH SECURITY

The security of pass-env is bounded by the integrity of your GPG key and
password store.  If an attacker can write to an entry in your password store
(via a compromised GPG key, a shared store, or a symlink attack), they can
inject arbitrary environment variables or execute commands when you load that
entry.

Both
.BR "pass env set" " and " "pass env run"
are affected by a compromised store.  The
.B set
subcommand evaluates entry content as shell code, while
.B run
exports variables directly into a subprocess.  A malicious entry can set
dangerous variables like
.BR PATH " or " LD_PRELOAD
to hijack the spawned process.

.B "pass env run"
avoids
.B eval
and scopes variables to a subshell (nothing leaks into the calling shell),
but it does not protect against a hostile store.  Its advantage is cleanup
and isolation, not immunity to tampered entries.

To reduce risk, pass-env refuses to set well-known dangerous environment
variables (e.g.,
.BR PATH ,
.BR LD_PRELOAD ,
.BR PROMPT_COMMAND ,
.BR BASH_ENV )
from entries.  This is defense-in-depth and not a substitute for store
integrity.

Decrypted pass entry content is held as a bash variable during parsing.
Bash provides no mechanism to zero memory on
.BR unset .
On Linux, the decrypted values remain in the process's virtual memory until
reclaimed and are readable by same-user processes via
.IR /proc/<pid>/mem .

.SH ENVIRONMENT

.TP
.B PASSWORD_STORE_DIR
Root directory of the password store.  Defaults to
.IR ~/.password-store .

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pass (1),
.BR gpg (1),
.BR fzf (1)

